INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_5_all_1774922262689', 'A data science team is preparing a large, messy dataset for training a new machine learning model. The dataset contains missing values, inconsistent formats, and outliers. They need to efficiently clean and transform this data before feature engineering to improve model performance. Which AWS service provides the LEAST operational overhead for this task?', '["Use Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler to visually prepare and transform the data.","Write custom Python scripts on an Amazon EC2 instance to clean and preprocess the data.","Upload the raw data to Amazon S3 and manually inspect and edit files using a text editor.","Utilize AWS Glue to run Apache Spark jobs for data transformation, managed by the data engineering team."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) uses Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler, which provides a visual interface and built-in transformations for data preparation, significantly reducing the operational overhead compared to manual coding or managing infrastructure. Option B (incorrect) requires managing an Amazon EC2 instance and writing extensive custom code, which increases operational overhead and development time. Option C (incorrect) is a highly manual and inefficient process for large datasets, making it impractical and high in operational effort. Option D (incorrect) while effective for large-scale ETL, AWS Glue typically requires more setup and configuration compared to the streamlined experience of SageMaker Data Wrangler for a data science team focused on model preparation, thus having higher operational overhead for this specific use case.', 5, 'm_5_3', 1, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_5_all_1774922262689', 'A company has deployed an Amazon SageMaker endpoint for a real-time fraud detection model. Over several months, the model''s prediction accuracy has started to decline due to shifts in transaction patterns. The team needs a proactive way to detect this degradation and ensure the model remains effective. Which approach should they implement?', '["Manually retrain the model every quarter regardless of performance.","Implement Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor to detect data and model quality drift.","Set up Amazon CloudWatch alarms to monitor the endpoint''s invocation count and latency.","Develop a custom solution using AWS Lambda to periodically run inference on historical data."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) involves implementing Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor, which is specifically designed to continuously monitor the quality of machine learning models in production, detecting data drift, concept drift, and other performance issues. This allows for proactive intervention and ensures continuous high performance. Option A (incorrect) is a reactive and inefficient approach; retraining on a fixed schedule without monitoring model performance directly violates the principle of continuous monitoring and may lead to prolonged periods of degraded performance (anti-pattern: treating model deployment as the end of the ML lifecycle). Option C (incorrect) monitors operational metrics like invocation count and latency, which are important for endpoint health but do not directly indicate model performance degradation or data drift. Option D (incorrect) would require significant custom development and operational overhead to replicate the specialized capabilities of Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor.', 5, 'm_5_3', 2, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_5_all_1774922262689', 'A medical research institution developed an ML model to classify rare disease cases. The dataset is highly imbalanced, with only 1% of samples representing positive cases. The initial model achieved 99% accuracy, but doctors report many missed positive cases. Which set of metrics should the team prioritize to accurately evaluate the model''s effectiveness for the rare disease?', '["Accuracy and Root Mean Square Error (RMSE).","Mean Absolute Error (MAE) and R-squared.","Precision, Recall, and F1 Score.","Training loss and validation loss."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) identifies Precision, Recall, and F1 Score as the most appropriate metrics for evaluating a classification model with highly imbalanced datasets. High accuracy alone can be misleading in such scenarios (anti-pattern: optimizing for accuracy alone regardless of class imbalance), as a model can achieve high accuracy by simply predicting the majority class. Precision measures the proportion of true positive predictions that were actually correct, Recall measures the proportion of actual positive cases that were correctly identified, and F1 Score is the harmonic mean of precision and recall, providing a balanced measure. Option A (incorrect) includes Accuracy, which is misleading for imbalanced data, and RMSE, which is a regression metric. Option B (incorrect) lists MAE and R-squared, which are both regression metrics, not suitable for classification tasks. Option D (incorrect) refers to training and validation loss, which are used during the training phase to monitor model convergence but are not primary evaluation metrics for production model performance.', 5, 'm_5_3', 3, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_5_all_1774922262689', 'An e-commerce company needs to generate personalized product recommendations for all its 10 million active users once every 24 hours. The recommendations do not need to be real-time, but the process must be cost-effective and scalable for large datasets. Which AWS service and inference method should be used?', '["Deploy an Amazon SageMaker real-time endpoint for each user''s recommendation request.","Use AWS Lambda functions to process user data and generate recommendations individually.","Utilize Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform to process the entire user dataset at once.","Manually run inference scripts on an Amazon EC2 instance daily to generate recommendations."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) recommends using Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform. This service is designed for large-scale, asynchronous inference on entire datasets, making it highly cost-effective and scalable for scenarios where real-time predictions are not required, such as daily recommendation generation. Option A (incorrect) involves deploying a real-time endpoint, which is optimized for low-latency, on-demand predictions. Using it for 10 million users daily would be significantly more expensive and less efficient than a batch process. Option B (incorrect) would incur high costs and potentially hit concurrency limits if 10 million Lambda functions are invoked simultaneously for a single batch job. Option D (incorrect) requires manual intervention, lacks scalability, and increases operational overhead, making it unsuitable for a large, recurring task.', 5, 'm_5_3', 4, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_5_all_1774922262689', 'A data scientist has trained a machine learning model that achieves nearly perfect accuracy on the training dataset but performs poorly when tested with new, unseen customer data. This indicates a common problem in the ML development lifecycle. What is the most likely issue with the model?', '["The model has overfit the training data.","The model is underfit and too simple.","The dataset contains too many features.","The model''s learning rate is too high."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) states that the model has overfit the training data. Overfitting occurs when a model learns the training data too well, including noise and specific patterns, leading to excellent performance on the training set but poor generalization to new, unseen data. Option B (incorrect) describes underfitting, where a model is too simple to capture the underlying patterns in the data, resulting in poor performance on both training and test data. Option C (incorrect) having too many features can contribute to overfitting, but it is a cause, not the direct description of the model''s state. Option D (incorrect) a high learning rate can lead to unstable training and divergence, but it doesn''t directly describe the symptom of high training accuracy and low test accuracy.', 5, 'm_5_3', 5, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_5_all_1774922262689', 'A startup is building its first machine learning solution to analyze customer feedback. They are defining the sequential steps required to take raw data, build a model, and deploy it for predictions. Which sequence correctly represents the typical stages of the ML development lifecycle?', '["Model training, data collection, feature engineering, deployment, evaluation, monitoring.","Data collection, preprocessing, feature engineering, model training, evaluation, deployment, monitoring.","Deployment, model training, data collection, evaluation, preprocessing, feature engineering, monitoring.","Evaluation, data collection, model training, preprocessing, deployment, feature engineering, monitoring."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) accurately outlines the typical stages of the ML development lifecycle: Data collection (gathering raw data), preprocessing (cleaning and transforming data), feature engineering (creating relevant features), model training (teaching the model), evaluation (assessing model performance), deployment (making the model available), and monitoring (tracking performance in production). Option A (incorrect) presents a scrambled order, starting with training before data collection and preprocessing. Option C (incorrect) starts with deployment, which is a final stage, and mixes up the order of other crucial steps. Option D (incorrect) also begins with evaluation and has an incorrect sequence of stages, making it an impractical and inefficient approach to ML development.', 5, 'm_5_3', 6, NULL, NULL);

