INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_5_all_1774922262689', 'A small e-commerce company wants to implement a simple chatbot to answer frequently asked questions (FAQs) about their products. The responses need to be quick and cost-effective, as the volume of queries is moderate. The questions are straightforward and do not require complex reasoning or external tool use. Which Amazon Bedrock foundation model selection strategy is MOST appropriate for this use case, prioritizing cost-effectiveness and low latency?', '["Choose a smaller, more specialized foundation model available on Amazon Bedrock that is optimized for text generation and classification.","Select the largest, most powerful general-purpose foundation model on Amazon Bedrock to ensure the highest possible accuracy for all queries.","Opt for a multi-modal foundation model to handle potential image-based queries, even though the current requirement is text-only.","Fine-tune a custom foundation model on their entire product catalog to achieve highly specific and accurate responses for every possible question."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is the most appropriate strategy. For simple FAQ chatbots with moderate query volumes, a smaller, specialized foundation model offers a balance of cost-effectiveness and low latency while still providing sufficient accuracy for straightforward text generation and classification tasks. Option B (incorrect) is an anti-pattern (ap-3.1-001). Choosing the largest, most powerful model for every task significantly increases cost per token and latency, which is unnecessary for simple FAQs and goes against the ''cost-effectiveness and low latency'' constraint. Option C (incorrect) selecting a multi-modal model is not required for a text-only FAQ chatbot and would introduce unnecessary complexity and cost. Option D (incorrect) fine-tuning a custom model is a more involved and costly process, suitable for highly specific domain knowledge or unique response styles, but it is overkill and not the ''MOST appropriate'' for simple FAQs, especially when prioritizing cost-effectiveness.', 7, 'm_7_1', 9, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_5_all_1774922262689', 'A research institution needs to build an internal knowledge retrieval system for its vast collection of scientific papers. Users should be able to ask natural language questions and receive precise answers grounded in the content of these documents, without the foundation model hallucinating information. The institution wants to leverage Amazon Bedrock for this, minimizing the need for model fine-tuning. Which architectural pattern should the Solutions Architect recommend?', '["Implement a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) pattern using Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases, linking to the scientific papers.","Fine-tune a large language model on the entire dataset of scientific papers to embed all knowledge directly into the model''s parameters.","Use prompt engineering with zero-shot prompting to instruct a general-purpose foundation model to answer questions based on implicit knowledge.","Develop a custom search engine using Amazon OpenSearch Service and present the top search results directly to the user without a foundation model."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) describes the Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) pattern with Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases. This approach allows the foundation model to retrieve relevant information from the scientific papers and use it to generate grounded answers, significantly reducing hallucinations and ensuring accuracy without extensive fine-tuning. Option B (incorrect) fine-tuning a large language model on a vast dataset is a very expensive and time-consuming process, and it''s not the most efficient way to ensure grounded answers from frequently updated or very large external knowledge bases. Option C (incorrect) relying on zero-shot prompting with a general-purpose model would likely lead to hallucinations and inaccurate answers, as the model would not have direct access to the specific scientific papers. Option D (incorrect) while a custom search engine could retrieve documents, it would not provide natural language answers or summaries, failing to meet the requirement for a ''knowledge retrieval system'' that leverages foundation models.', 7, 'm_7_1', 10, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_5_all_1774922262689', 'A financial services company wants to automate complex, multi-step customer service workflows, such as processing loan applications or resolving billing disputes. These workflows involve interacting with several internal systems (e.g., CRM, payment gateway) and often require conditional logic based on customer input. The company aims to use Amazon Bedrock to orchestrate these tasks. Which Amazon Bedrock feature is best suited for this requirement?', '["Utilize Amazon Bedrock Agents to orchestrate multi-step tasks by defining actions, tools, and business logic.","Employ a single, large foundation model with advanced chain-of-thought prompting to handle the entire workflow within one prompt.","Implement Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases to store all internal system documentation, allowing the model to retrieve instructions.","Develop a series of interconnected AWS Lambda functions, each calling a foundation model and handling a single step of the workflow."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is the ideal solution. Amazon Bedrock Agents are specifically designed to orchestrate multi-step tasks, interact with external systems (tools), and apply business logic, making them perfect for automating complex workflows like loan applications or billing disputes. Option B (incorrect) attempting to handle complex, multi-step workflows with conditional logic using a single prompt, even with chain-of-thought, is highly impractical and prone to errors. Foundation models are not designed for robust, stateful orchestration of external systems through prompting alone. Option C (incorrect) Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases are for information retrieval and grounding, not for orchestrating actions or interacting with external systems. Option D (incorrect) while technically possible, developing a series of interconnected Lambda functions to manage the workflow would involve significant custom development and operational overhead, effectively reinventing the orchestration capabilities that Amazon Bedrock Agents provide out-of-the-box.', 7, 'm_7_1', 11, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_5_all_1774922262689', 'A media company is developing an application that needs to analyze user-submitted images and generate descriptive captions for them. The captions should not only describe the visual content but also infer context where possible. The company wants to use a single foundation model on Amazon Bedrock for this task. Which type of model is required to fulfill this multi-modal input requirement?', '["A text-to-text foundation model, with a separate image recognition service to extract features before text generation.","A multi-modal foundation model that can process both image and text inputs to generate text outputs.","A text-to-image foundation model, which can generate images from textual descriptions.","A specialized image classification model, which can categorize images but not generate descriptive captions."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) describes a multi-modal foundation model. These models are specifically designed to process and understand information from multiple modalities, such as images and text, and generate outputs in another modality, like text captions from image inputs. Option A (incorrect) using a text-to-text model with a separate image recognition service would require complex integration and feature engineering, which defeats the purpose of leveraging a single foundation model for multi-modal input. Option C (incorrect) a text-to-image foundation model generates images from text, which is the opposite of the required functionality. Option D (incorrect) a specialized image classification model can only categorize images, not generate descriptive captions, failing to meet the core requirement of generating text descriptions.', 7, 'm_7_1', 12, NULL, NULL);

