INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_18_all_1774925287644', 'A healthcare company stores patient records in an Amazon S3 bucket. Due to strict regulatory compliance, all data must be encrypted at rest using customer-managed keys (CMKs) with full control over key rotation and an audit trail of key usage. The solution must also integrate seamlessly with S3. Which encryption option should the Solutions Architect recommend for the S3 bucket?', '["Server-Side Encryption with Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3) for automatic encryption.","Client-Side Encryption (CSE) where the application encrypts data before uploading to S3 using a custom key management system.","Server-Side Encryption with AWS Key Management Service (SSE-KMS) using a customer-managed key (CMK) for encryption.","Server-Side Encryption with Customer-Provided Keys (SSE-C) where the customer provides encryption keys for each object."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the most appropriate solution. Server-Side Encryption with AWS Key Management Service (SSE-KMS) allows S3 to encrypt objects using AWS KMS. When using a customer-managed key (CMK) in KMS, the customer has full control over the key, including defining key policies, enabling automatic key rotation, and auditing key usage through AWS CloudTrail, which meets the compliance requirements. Option A (incorrect) uses AWS-managed keys (SSE-S3), which do not provide customer control over key rotation or an audit trail of key usage, failing to meet the compliance requirement for customer-managed keys, aligning with anti-pattern [ap-1.3-001]. Option B (incorrect) requires significant custom development and management of a custom key management system, increasing operational overhead and complexity. Option D (incorrect) requires the customer to provide an encryption key with every S3 API request, which can be cumbersome to manage and does not provide the centralized key management and auditability of KMS CMKs.', 18, 'm_18_3', 16, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_18_all_1774925287644', 'A media company needs to store video archives in Amazon S3 for regulatory compliance. The requirement is to ensure that once a video is uploaded, it cannot be deleted or modified for a period of 7 years. The solution must prevent accidental or malicious deletion and support legal holds. Which S3 feature combination should be implemented?', '["Enable S3 Versioning on the bucket and then apply an S3 Lifecycle policy to transition objects to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 7 years.","Enable S3 Object Lock in WORM (Write Once Read Many) mode with a Governance retention period of 7 years, and ensure S3 Versioning is enabled.","Configure an S3 bucket policy to deny the ''s3:DeleteObject'' action for all users and roles, and set a legal hold manually for each object.","Use S3 Replication to copy objects to a different bucket in another AWS account, relying on the destination bucket for immutability."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most suitable solution. S3 Object Lock, when configured in WORM (Write Once Read Many) mode, prevents objects from being deleted or overwritten for a fixed amount of time (retention period) or indefinitely (legal hold). It requires S3 Versioning to be enabled on the bucket to function correctly. A Governance retention period allows certain users with special permissions to override the lock, while Compliance mode makes it impossible for anyone, including the root user, to override. This meets the immutability requirement. Option A (incorrect) enables versioning and transitions to Glacier, but a lifecycle policy alone does not prevent deletion or modification during the 7-year active period. Versioning is a prerequisite for Object Lock, but not sufficient on its own for immutability without Object Lock. This aligns with anti-pattern [ap-1.3-002] if Object Lock is attempted without versioning. Option C (incorrect) is insufficient because a bucket policy can be modified or deleted by an administrator, and manual legal holds are not scalable or robust. Option D (incorrect) provides redundancy but does not inherently provide immutability or protection against deletion/modification in the source or destination bucket without additional controls like Object Lock.', 18, 'm_18_3', 17, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_18_all_1774925287644', 'A large enterprise has multiple AWS accounts for different departments and environments (e.g., Development, Staging, Production). Each account uses various AWS services that require encryption at rest. The security team needs a strategy for managing encryption keys that ensures strong isolation, auditability, and limits the blast radius if a key is compromised. Which AWS Key Management Service (KMS) strategy should be recommended?', '["Create a single AWS KMS customer-managed key (CMK) in a central security account and share it across all services and accounts.","Use AWS-managed keys (AWS-owned CMKs or AWS-managed CMKs) for all services, as they are fully managed by AWS.","Create separate AWS KMS customer-managed keys (CMKs) for each application or service, and ideally for each account, to limit the scope of compromise.","Implement AWS CloudHSM in a central account and use it to generate and manage all encryption keys for all services across all accounts."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the recommended strategy for strong isolation and limiting the blast radius. Creating separate AWS KMS customer-managed keys (CMKs) for each application or service, and ideally for each account, ensures that a compromise of one key only affects a limited scope of data. This approach enhances auditability and aligns with the principle of least privilege for keys. Option A (incorrect) is an anti-pattern [ap-1.3-003] because using a single KMS key across all services and accounts significantly increases the blast radius if that key is compromised, violating the principle of isolation. Option B (incorrect) uses AWS-managed keys, which do not provide the customer with control over key policies, rotation schedules, or direct audit trails of key usage, failing to meet the requirement for strong auditability and control. Option D (incorrect) provides the highest level of FIPS compliance and full key control but introduces significant operational overhead and cost compared to KMS, which is not necessary unless strict FIPS 140-2 Level 3 compliance is explicitly required for all keys, aligning with anti-pattern [ap-1.3-004] if KMS is sufficient.', 18, 'm_18_3', 18, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_18_all_1774925287644', 'A government agency requires cryptographic keys for highly sensitive data to be stored in dedicated, single-tenant hardware security modules (HSMs) that meet FIPS 140-2 Level 3 compliance. The agency needs full control over the keys, including direct access to the HSMs for key generation and management. Which AWS service is designed to meet these stringent requirements?', '["AWS Key Management Service (KMS) with customer-managed keys (CMKs).","AWS CloudHSM, providing dedicated hardware security modules in the AWS cloud.","AWS Secrets Manager for storing and managing cryptographic keys.","AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store SecureString for encrypted key storage."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the only AWS service that meets the requirement for dedicated, single-tenant hardware security modules (HSMs) with FIPS 140-2 Level 3 compliance and full customer control over keys. AWS CloudHSM provides dedicated HSM instances in the AWS cloud, allowing customers to generate and manage their own encryption keys within a FIPS 140-2 Level 3 validated environment. Option A (incorrect) provides FIPS 140-2 Level 2 compliance and is a multi-tenant service, where AWS manages the underlying HSMs, not providing the full control or dedicated hardware required, aligning with anti-pattern [ap-1.3-004]. Option C (incorrect) is for managing application secrets and credentials, not for direct management of cryptographic keys in dedicated HSMs. Option D (incorrect) is for storing sensitive configuration data and parameters, not for managing cryptographic keys in FIPS 140-2 Level 3 compliant HSMs.', 18, 'm_18_3', 19, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_18_all_1774925287644', 'A company needs to store application logs in Amazon S3 for auditing and compliance purposes. These logs must be retained for 5 years but are accessed infrequently after the first 30 days. The primary goal is to minimize storage costs while ensuring data availability for audit queries when needed. Which S3 feature should be used to achieve this with LEAST operational overhead?', '["Manually move objects from S3 Standard to S3 Glacier after 30 days using custom scripts.","Configure an S3 Lifecycle policy to transition objects from S3 Standard to S3 Standard-IA after 30 days, then to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 90 days, and finally delete after 5 years.","Store all logs directly in S3 Glacier Deep Archive from ingestion to minimize initial costs.","Use S3 Object Lock to prevent deletion of logs for 5 years and store them in S3 Standard."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most cost-effective and operationally efficient solution. An S3 Lifecycle policy automates the transition of objects between different S3 storage classes based on predefined rules, minimizing storage costs without manual intervention. Transitioning to S3 Standard-IA for infrequent access, then to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval for long-term archival, and finally deleting after 5 years aligns with the access patterns and retention requirements while optimizing costs. Option A (incorrect) requires manual intervention or custom scripts, which increases operational overhead. Option C (incorrect) would incur higher retrieval costs and longer retrieval times for the first 30 days and infrequent access period, making it unsuitable for logs that might be accessed within the first few months. Option D (incorrect) ensures immutability but does not optimize storage costs for infrequently accessed or archived data; S3 Standard is more expensive for long-term, infrequent access compared to Glacier storage classes.', 18, 'm_18_3', 20, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_18_all_1774925287644', 'A web application relies on a critical Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL database. The business requires a robust data recovery strategy that includes automated daily backups and the ability to perform point-in-time recovery (PITR) to any second within the last 7 days. The solution must have minimal impact on database performance. Which Amazon RDS feature should be configured?', '["Manually create database snapshots daily and store them in Amazon S3.","Enable Amazon RDS Automated Backups with a backup retention period of 7 days.","Set up a custom AWS Lambda function to export database dumps to Amazon S3 hourly.","Configure Amazon RDS Multi-AZ deployment for high availability, which inherently provides PITR."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most appropriate solution for automated daily backups and point-in-time recovery. Amazon RDS Automated Backups automatically create a full daily snapshot of your database and capture transaction logs (redo logs) throughout the day. With a 7-day retention period, you can restore your database to any specific second within that window, meeting the PITR requirement with minimal operational overhead and performance impact. Option A (incorrect) requires manual intervention or custom automation, increasing operational overhead and potentially missing the ''any second'' PITR capability. Option C (incorrect) involves custom development, incurs operational overhead, and might not provide the precise point-in-time recovery capability of RDS automated backups. Option D (incorrect) provides high availability and automatic failover, but it does not inherently provide point-in-time recovery; automated backups are still required for PITR, even with Multi-AZ.', 18, 'm_18_3', 21, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_18_all_1774925287644', 'A new microservices application needs to store sensitive configuration data, such as API keys for third-party services, and database connection strings. These secrets must be encrypted at rest, easily retrievable by the application, and not hardcoded into the application''s source code. The solution should also support versioning of secrets. Which AWS service is best suited for this purpose?', '["Store the secrets as plain text files on the Amazon EC2 instances running the microservices.","Use AWS Secrets Manager to store and retrieve the sensitive configuration data, leveraging its encryption and versioning capabilities.","Embed the secrets directly into the application''s Docker images or build artifacts.","Store the secrets in an Amazon S3 bucket with server-side encryption enabled, and grant the application S3 read access."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the best solution. AWS Secrets Manager is specifically designed for securely storing, managing, and retrieving sensitive information like API keys and database credentials. It encrypts secrets at rest, supports automatic rotation, and provides versioning, allowing applications to retrieve the latest secret dynamically without hardcoding. Option A (incorrect) is highly insecure as plain text files are easily accessible and not encrypted at rest. Option C (incorrect) hardcodes secrets into artifacts, making them difficult to rotate and risking exposure through version control or image registries. Option D (incorrect) is not ideal for managing application secrets; while S3 provides encryption, it lacks the dynamic retrieval, rotation, and versioning capabilities tailored for secrets management that AWS Secrets Manager offers.', 18, 'm_18_3', 22, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_18_all_1774925287644', 'A data analytics company needs to store large datasets in Amazon S3. These datasets contain personally identifiable information (PII) and must be automatically discovered and classified across all S3 buckets to ensure compliance. The security team requires a managed service that can identify sensitive data without manual intervention. Which AWS service should be used?', '["Configure AWS Config rules to monitor S3 bucket policies for PII access.","Enable Amazon Macie to automatically discover, classify, and report on sensitive data, including PII, in Amazon S3.","Use Amazon GuardDuty to detect suspicious activity related to S3 access patterns.","Implement AWS CloudTrail to log all S3 data events and then analyze logs for PII manually."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most appropriate solution. Amazon Macie is a fully managed data security and data privacy service that uses machine learning and pattern matching to discover, classify, and protect sensitive data in AWS, especially in Amazon S3. It can automatically identify PII and other sensitive information, providing insights into data security and compliance. Option A (incorrect) monitors configuration changes and compliance of S3 bucket policies, but it does not discover or classify the actual content of the data within the buckets for PII. Option C (incorrect) is a threat detection service that monitors for malicious activity and unauthorized behavior, not for discovering and classifying sensitive data content. Option D (incorrect) logs API calls and events, which can be used for auditing, but manually analyzing logs for PII is not an automated solution and would be highly inefficient for large datasets.', 18, 'm_18_3', 23, NULL, NULL);

